On Fri, Apr 30, 1999 at 11:11:29PM -0500, Wes Bauske wrote:
> Shaw tells me things are more thoughtful now on the Debian Alpha
> list if you're interested. As I said, I'll stick to RH unless
> something dire occurs, like RH decides to stop supporting Alphas!

Actually, I said nothing about the list being any different now
than it was, say, 9 months ago.  I just said I've never encountered any
"zealots" prohibiting anyone from freely discussing anything that
comes up.  

I don't remember spotting these "zealots."  I've been subscribed to 
both the Red Hat and Debian lists for the same period of time.  The 
Red Hat list has more traffic, but also more non-Alpha content (people
who don't know what "AXP" means, asking for Intel kernel compiles).  
It's not a big deal.  Overall, these lists have an excellent signal-to-
noise ratio.  I've had no problem with either list, that's why I found it
odd that people seem to be encountering these dangerous overzealous 
demons eager to thwart every attempt at discussion.  I understood
such a message as a distribution war in disguise, especially 
considering the original detractor doesn't seem to even READ the
Debian list any more.

Debian on the Alpha works great.  I've never had a problem with it.
I've heard Red Hat works well too.  Personally, I'm not very flattered
with either of the distribution layouts; Debian just made things
much easier for me when I had to install something on the Alpha.
I had a T1 and a floppy, and things worked out fine.  I'll be 
burning my own distribution CDs soon enough, but I still plan on reading
these mailing lists.  Often they're full of discussions of the latest
hardware support (those yummy 21264s), news of optimized software for
the Alpha, and general tricks and neat things to do with them.
