Well, after long last, I decided to try out my copy of Visual C++ 6.0 on
the OpenGL programs that Microsoft distributes.  I found that when using
software acceleration, they seem to run about twice as fast as the same
programs running under Linux, software acceleration.  The sample programs
are typically freely available, and many are also included with MESA.

Under Linux, I am using egcs-1.1.1 as a compiler, and MetroX as the X
server, the performance on OpenGL programs is indistinguishable from
XFree, except I can use two monitors. I think the lib is glibc 2.0, kernel
is 2.2.7, on an otherwise stock Red Hat 5.1 system.

On both OSs, I am using two Matrox Millennium display cards, on a dual
booting NT - Linux system, with a 21164A chip.

I was disappointed that Linux couldn't keep up in graphics speed.  Is
there anything I can do to tune MESA for better performace?  I am hoping
for a 'quick fix' as in changing configurations or patching, not rewriting
using assembly language.

Thanks.

Jeff DeMaagd


