> successful installation, tells me to remove floppy and hit enter.
> Done.

This is the boot disk you inserted correct?

> ERROR: failed to open device 0x200 (res 
> MILO: unknown filesystem on fd
> MILO: Failed to load kernel
> MILO> boot sda6:vmlinux.gz

Are you sure sda6 is the patition you labeled "/" ? 
If that is the correct partition your whole boot string should be

boot sda6:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/sda6
 
> EXT2-fs: unable to read superblock
> [MS-DOS FS Rel. 12,FAT 12,check=n,conv=b,uid=0,umask=022,bmap]

It looks like either the ext2 fs wasn't formated or it's pointing to one
of your FAT16 disks. Most likely it's the FAT16 .
 
> Perhaps I ought to know the significance of all this, but it is beyond
> me. I suspect there is something to do with the Jaz drive, but what?

No problem with the Jaz drive. I have an external scsi zip on the same
system.

> Suggestions? Yes, using the Milo and boot images from the CD for lx164,
> and could not find any that were newer than these. did try a recent test
> milo on the last go-round, no difference. also using the new ramdisk
> image from the RH errata page.
> Thanks in advance,
> Aron


It really sounds like you're booting the wrong partition. The easiest way
to find out which sda# is your Linux partiton try this at the milo prompt

Using the boot disk you made for the installation.

boot fd0:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/sda# 

and keep trying till you hit the right partiton. 

You can also mount drives in milo and see their contents. Hit help while
in milo to see what commands you have available. Find the right paritition
with the kernel on it and then boot it.

Good Luck!

