Normal disclaimer:  I searched the archive as much as I could, and didn't 
find anything applicable.  Sorry if I missed anything.

So my Alphastation 200 that I'd been running as a web/email server for the 
last year and a half or so had its power supply die on me.   And to make a
long story short, I'm currently trying to access the data that (I hope) is
still on the disk that I was running there.

The problem is that my home Linux box, which is on an x86 system, can't seem
to read the disklabel on that drive that I pulled from the Alpha.  I was 
booting the Alphastation using the SRM console, so I had used the 'minlabel'
program to partition (or label, I suppose) the disk.  Making sure that UFS
and BSD disklabel support was in my kernel didn't help; the best response that
I got was getting a corrupted partition table error when I had the SMD 
disklabel option set.

My bit of research that I've done between then and sending this message has 
turned up some referencs to the disklabel in question being a BSD disklabel
(which, since my x86 couldn't read the disk, would make me think that the
power supply took my disk out with it when it died), and some other 
mentions of the OSF/DecUNIX disklabel being a related but different animal
(which might mean that my disk is still ok).

So the question is:  has anyone ever accessed an Alpha Linux disklabeled
disk under an x86 system?  If so, how?  Should it have worked just with 
support for BSD disklabels, or is there something else that I have to do?

Any help would be greatly appreciated.

-allen petersen
allen@suberic.net (currently existing as a VIF...)




